WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.740
President Donald Trump has been impeached
again, making him the only president in history

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.859
to be impeached two times.

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:15.610
And in fact, this has been a presidency rife
with firsts, but they're all pretty bad.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.570
And we're going to talk about that in a subsequent
segment.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.050
But, yes, Donald Trump becoming the first
president to be impeached twice the House

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:31.560
this time voting on just a single charge,
citing Trump's role in inciting that mob that

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.879
violently went to the Capitol on January 6th.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:40.730
And Donald Trump now will face a Senate trial
that could disqualify him from future office,

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:45.500
although that Senate trial by every indication
isn't going to happen until Joe Biden assumes

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:53.730
the presidency, 10 Republicans joined Democrats
in the House of Representatives in voting

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.230
yesterday for the impeachment of Donald Trump.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:05.230
This was now a capital much more militarized,
much more fortified by law enforcement and

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:10.750
military as a result of what took place on
January 6th.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.920
Let's go to the moment that it happened.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.410
Nancy Pelosi announcing the results for the
second time during Donald Trump's presidency

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:23.780
that indeed the president has been impeached.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:30.750
On this vote, the yeas are 232, the nays are
197.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:37.229
The resolution is adopted without objection,
the motion to reconsider is laid upon the

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:38.729
table.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:45.880
OK, so going forward, when we say the Trump
impeachment, we are going to need to clarify

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:52.630
which of the two Trump impeachments we are
referring to and notably different this time

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:58.009
versus the House vote about a year ago, a
little more than a year ago in impeaching

00:01:58.009 --> 00:02:03.840
Donald Trump, the first time, 10 Republicans
joining the Democrats, Congressman CatCo,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:13.900
Liz Cheney, Kinzinger Upton, Herrera Butler,
new House Mayor Gonzalez, Rice and Valadao

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.450
joining the Democratic Party.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:21.750
I don't think that these Republicans are particularly
valiant or brave, although we will talk on

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:27.790
the bonus show today about how Liz Cheney
is facing calls to resign because she voted

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.819
for the impeachment of Donald Trump.

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:37.569
Can you imagine resign because you did the
thing that is your responsibility and duty

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:40.010
to do as a member of the House of Representatives?

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.840
Very backwards, very backwards calls for resignation.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.209
We are hearing from Republicans.

00:02:46.209 --> 00:02:51.950
And this is going to be yet another stain
on Donald Trump's legacy only one week before

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.580
leaving office to the day.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:03:02.769
And we are I mean, listen, this this we talk
we've talked a lot over the last four years.

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:08.209
As we've fallen further and further down in
terms of global respectability, and as the

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:13.050
shame has grown and the humiliation has grown,
we've talked about what will it take to get

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.440
back to where we were.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.400
And for many of us, where we were wasn't that
great.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.800
I mean, OK, diplomatically, the United States
was more respected around the world under

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.690
Barack Obama and previous presidencies, but
we still were falling behind in dealing with

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:28.690
climate.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.319
We still weren't getting health care to everybody.

00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:32.970
Even as one of the richest countries in the
world.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.080
We still had hunger.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.209
We still had unemployment.

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:40.439
We were far better off than we are now, far
more respected.

00:03:40.439 --> 00:03:44.580
But we still had serious problems to solve
and we cannot.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:50.960
It is unreasonable to expect that after this
level of global shame and domestic shame,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.700
just because the Trump is don't feel ashamed
doesn't mean it's not significant shame.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.580
It took four years for this to culminate in
Donald Trump's second impeachment and this

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.670
riotous mob that he incited.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.650
It's going to take more than six months to
make significant headway in getting out of

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:06.650
it.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.340
And hopefully our historical allies will will
forgive us.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.750
That's really the question.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.900
And there is going to be a lot of work digging
out from this.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:23.470
Even many Republicans in House speeches yesterday
in the anticipation of this vote, including

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:30.360
Representative Kevin McCarthy, conceding that,
yes, Donald Trump was to blame for what happened

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.250
at the Capitol last week.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:39.120
But many continuing to insist that despite
that, it is not impeachable.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.090
So we are all, of course, disappointed that
there are unlikely to be greater consequences

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:51.200
for Donald Trump if things go incredibly well
and the Senate votes to convict when they

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:58.781
likely in February, March, April, we don't
know, convene for the impeachment trial of

00:04:58.781 --> 00:05:01.390
Trump if they were to convict.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:07.290
It is a possibility that Trump will face the
consequence of not being able to run again.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.790
But we all wish there had been more consequences.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:15.360
And I think maybe the biggest takeaway is,
were it not for you getting out and voting

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.110
in 2018 to take that majority away in the
House of Representatives from Republicans,

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:25.710
were it not for you voting in 2020 to remove
Donald Trump and to give Democrats a Senate

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.550
majority, we wouldn't even be seeing a lot
of what we've seen.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:33.220
The first impeachment wouldn't have happened
were it not for the twenty eighteen vote,

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.220
the second impeachment.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:39.720
I mean, we'd be dealing with a different situation,
let's put it that way, were it not for taking

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.200
the White House and the Senate in in November
and in the runoff elections in Georgia in

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:45.200
January.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.110
So there are successes here, but we all wish
that there had been more consequences and

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:55.330
earlier on for what has been arguably the
most atrocious presidency in modern political

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:55.800
history

